\title{Codiscrete One-Object Bicategories and the\\Associativity Obstruction to Strictness}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a uniform construction of a one-object bicategory from any unital magma. The $1$-cells are the magma elements, composition is magma multiplication, and every pair of $1$-cells is connected by a unique $2$-cell. Codiscreteness makes all $2$-cells invertible and forces the pentagon, triangle, whiskering, and interchange laws. We characterize strictness on the fixed data: the bicategory admits a strict structure if and only if multiplication is associative. Thus the associator absorbs arbitrary associativity defects bicategorically, while strictness detects them exactly. A concrete multiplication $\star$ on $\mathbb N$ satisfies $(1\star1)\star1=5$ and $1\star(1\star1)=7$; its associated bicategory is coherent and genuinely non-strict. We also give finite algorithms for associativity testing and defect enumeration, and discuss extensions to cohomological obstructions and strictification up to biequivalence.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Associativity permits parentheses to be omitted. A binary operation $*$ is associative when
\[
(a*b)*c=a*(b*c)
\]
for all triples. Ordinary categories impose this equality on composition. Bicategories weaken the equation without abandoning control: the two bracketings need not be equal as $1$-cells, but they must be connected by a specified invertible $2$-cell, the associator. Associators satisfy the pentagon coherence law, while identity $1$-cells are governed by left and right unitors satisfying a triangle law.

This distinction between equality and coherent equivalence is especially transparent in the one-object case, where $1$-cell composition resembles multiplication. We ask how little algebra is required to support a bicategory. A binary operation with a two-sided unit is enough, provided the hom-category is codiscrete.

Let $(M,*,e)$ be a unital magma, with no associativity hypothesis. Construct a single-object system whose $1$-cells are elements of $M$, and declare exactly one $2$-cell between each ordered pair of $1$-cells. Every structural comparison then exists uniquely, including
\[
\alpha_{a,b,c}:(a*b)*c\Rightarrow a*(b*c).
\]
All coherence equations follow from uniqueness. Nevertheless, strictness cannot manufacture endpoint equalities. We prove the exact criterion
\[
\text{strictness on fixed data}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\text{associativity of }*.
\]
Here ``on fixed data'' means retaining the given $1$-cells and multiplication. It is distinct from replacement by a biequivalent strict $2$-category.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Unital magma]
A \emph{unital magma} is a triple $(M,*,e)$ consisting of a set $M$, a binary operation $*:M\times M\to M$, and $e\in M$ such that
\[
e*a=a,\qquad a*e=a
\]
for every $a\in M$. A monoid is a unital magma satisfying $(a*b)*c=a*(b*c)$ for all $a,b,c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Associativity defect]
An associativity defect is a triple $(a,b,c)\in M^3$ such that
\[
(a*b)*c\ne a*(b*c).
\]
Its endpoint defect is the ordered pair $\delta(a,b,c)=((a*b)*c,a*(b*c))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Codiscrete category]
Given a set $S$, its codiscrete category has the elements of $S$ as objects and exactly one morphism $x\to y$ for every ordered pair $(x,y)$.
\end{definition}

Identities and composition are forced by uniqueness. Every arrow is invertible: the unique reverse arrow composes with it to the unique endomorphism at either endpoint, namely the identity.

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness of parallel arrows]
In a codiscrete category, any two arrows with the same source and target are equal. Consequently, any two isomorphisms with the same source and target are equal.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each hom-set has exactly one element. An isomorphism is determined by its forward arrow, which is unique.
\end{proof}

A bicategory has objects, $1$-cells, and $2$-cells between parallel $1$-cells. Each pair of objects has a hom-category. There are identity $1$-cells, composition, vertical composition of $2$-cells, and left and right whiskering. For composable $f,g,h$, the associator is
\[
\alpha_{f,g,h}:(f\circ g)\circ h\Rightarrow f\circ(g\circ h),
\]
and for $f:A\to B$ the unitors are
\[
\lambda_f:1_A\circ f\Rightarrow f,
\qquad
\rho_f:f\circ1_B\Rightarrow f.
\]
These data satisfy functoriality, interchange, naturality, the pentagon law, and the triangle law.

\begin{definition}[Strictness on fixed data]
A bicategory is strict on its fixed data if its existing composition satisfies literal identity and associativity equalities and its unitors and associator coincide with the canonical isomorphisms induced by those equalities.
\end{definition}

\section{The codiscrete construction}

Fix a unital magma $(M,*,e)$.

\begin{construction}
Take one object $\bullet$. Let the $1$-cells $\bullet\to\bullet$ be the elements of $M$, let the identity be $e$, and define composition by $a\circ b=a*b$. Between every $a,b\in M$, place exactly one $2$-cell $a\Rightarrow b$. Define all operations on $2$-cells, all unitors, and all associators as the unique arrows with the required endpoints.
\end{construction}

\begin{lemma}[Invertibility]
Every $2$-cell in the construction is invertible. In particular, all associators and unitors are isomorphisms.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For the unique $u:a\Rightarrow b$, let $v:b\Rightarrow a$ be the unique reverse arrow. Both $v\circ u$ and $u\circ v$ are the unique endomorphisms at their endpoints, hence identities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Codiscrete Bicategory Theorem]
Every unital magma $(M,*,e)$ determines a one-object bicategory by the construction above, whether or not $*$ is associative.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The hom-category is codiscrete, so its identities and vertical composition satisfy the category laws. Every operation on $2$-cells is uniquely determined by its endpoints. The identity $1$-cell laws follow from $e*a=a$ and $a*e=a$.

For $a,b,c\in M$, choose the unique isomorphism
\[
\alpha_{a,b,c}:(a*b)*c\Rightarrow a*(b*c)
\]
as associator. It exists even when its endpoints differ. Choose the unique isomorphisms
\[
\lambda_a:e*a\Rightarrow a,
\qquad
\rho_a:a*e\Rightarrow a
\]
as unitors. Every remaining bicategory axiom is an equality of parallel $2$-cells, including whiskering identities, interchange, naturality, pentagon, and triangle. Such arrows agree by uniqueness.
\end{proof}

For four $1$-cells, the pentagon compares two composites from $((a*b)*c)*d$ to $a*(b*(c*d))$. Intermediate bracketings may be distinct elements of $M$, but the two boundary routes are parallel $2$-cells. The triangle is handled identically.

\begin{corollary}[Automatic coherence]
Every well-typed diagram of $2$-cells in the codiscrete construction commutes.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Two paths with common endpoints yield parallel $2$-cells, and there is exactly one such cell.
\end{proof}

\section{Strictness and associativity}

\begin{proposition}[Necessity]
If the codiscrete bicategory associated with $(M,*,e)$ admits a strict structure on its fixed data, then $*$ is associative.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Strictness supplies the literal equality $(a*b)*c=a*(b*c)$ for every triple of $1$-cells. The $1$-cells are precisely the elements of $M$, and composition is precisely $*$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Sufficiency]
If $*$ is associative, then the associated codiscrete bicategory admits a strict structure on its fixed data.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The identity equalities are the magma unit axioms, and the associativity equality is the hypothesis. The existing unitors and associator must also agree with the isomorphisms induced by these equalities. In each case both candidates have identical endpoints in a codiscrete hom-category, so uniqueness makes them equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strictness Characterization]
The codiscrete one-object bicategory associated with a unital magma $(M,*,e)$ admits a strict structure on the fixed $1$-cells and fixed composition if and only if $M$ is associative.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine necessity and sufficiency.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Monoid case]
Every monoid gives rise to a strict codiscrete one-object bicategory.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Defect obstruction]
If a unital magma has one associativity defect, its codiscrete bicategory is coherent but has no strict structure on its fixed data.
\end{corollary}

The two directions use different mechanisms. Necessity extracts an algebraic equation from strictness. Sufficiency uses that equation together with uniqueness of comparison maps. Without codiscreteness, associativity need not force chosen structural isomorphisms to be equality-induced.

\section{A concrete genuinely non-strict example}

Define $\star$ on $\mathbb N$ by
\[
a\star b=
\begin{cases}
b,&a=0,\\
a,&b=0,\\
a+2b,&a>0\text{ and }b>0.
\end{cases}
\]

\begin{lemma}[Unit law]
The element $0$ is a two-sided unit for $\star$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The first branch gives $0\star b=b$. For positive $a$, the second gives $a\star0=a$; the remaining case is immediate.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Explicit defect]
The triple $(1,1,1)$ is an associativity defect, with
\[
(1\star1)\star1=5,
\qquad
1\star(1\star1)=7.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
One has $1\star1=3$, hence $3\star1=5$, while $1\star3=7$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Concrete Non-Strict Bicategory]
The codiscrete bicategory associated with $(\mathbb N,\star,0)$ is coherent and genuinely non-strict on its fixed data. Its associator at $(1,1,1)$ is the unique invertible $2$-cell $5\Rightarrow7$, and no strict structure can retain the same $1$-cells and composition.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The unit law enables the codiscrete construction. The explicit defect proves nonassociativity, so the Strictness Characterization rules out strictness.
\end{proof}

For positive $a,b,c$, expansion yields
\[
(a\star b)\star c=a+2b+2c,
\qquad
a\star(b\star c)=a+2b+4c.
\]

\begin{proposition}[Positive defect formula]
If $a,b,c>0$, then
\[
a\star(b\star c)-((a\star b)\star c)=2c>0.
\]
Thus every positive triple is an associativity defect.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Expand both products using the positive-input branch and subtract.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

Suppose $M$ is finite of cardinality $n$, with constant-time multiplication-table access.

\begin{algorithm}[Exhaustive strictness test]
For each $(a,b,c)\in M^3$, compute $L=(a*b)*c$ and $R=a*(b*c)$. If $L\ne R$, return ``non-strict'' with certificate $(a,b,c,L,R)$. If all triples pass, return ``strict.''
\end{algorithm}

\begin{proposition}[Correctness]
The exhaustive test returns ``strict'' exactly when the associated codiscrete bicategory admits a strict structure.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The test accepts exactly when multiplication is associative; apply the Strictness Characterization.
\end{proof}

The worst-case running time is $O(n^3)$ and auxiliary space is $O(1)$. Early termination may be much faster in nonassociative examples.

A defect census records $(a,b,c,L,R)$ for every failed triple. It takes $O(n^3)$ time and $O(k)$ output space, where $k\le n^3$ is the number of defects. The ratio $k/n^3$ measures defect frequency, although a single defect already prevents strictness.

A coherence-path simulator may compute the five parenthesizations
\[
((a*b)*c)*d,\quad (a*(b*c))*d,\quad (a*b)*(c*d),\quad
a*((b*c)*d),\quad a*(b*(c*d)).
\]
These values can differ. The pentagon nevertheless commutes because both routes between its extreme endpoints are the unique parallel $2$-cell. Numerical endpoint equality is neither needed nor asserted.

\section{Interpretation and applications}

The construction isolates the role of the associator. It does not assert equality of source and target $1$-cells; it supplies a reversible comparison. Thus $5$ and $7$ remain distinct natural numbers despite the invertible $2$-cell between the corresponding processes.

A unital magma can model processes whose combination has an identity but whose grouping affects outcomes. The codiscrete model gives a baseline two-dimensional calculus in which all outcomes are canonically intertransformable. Possible interpretations include workflow aggregation, syntax trees, stateful pipelines, and coarse models of distributed composition. The codiscrete assumption is deliberately strong, but it cleanly separates endpoint behavior from coherence.

Binary expressions correspond to parenthesized trees. Associators rotate one tree into another, and the rotation graph for four factors is a pentagon. Unique comparisons make every rebracketing path equivalent even when evaluation in the magma is not invariant under rotation.

The obstruction proved here concerns fixed data. It does not exclude replacement by a biequivalent strict $2$-category. Strict equality of a presentation and higher-categorical equivalence are separate notions.

\section{Discussion and future work}

Codiscreteness simultaneously guarantees existence, invertibility, and uniqueness of every $2$-cell. Coherence is therefore automatic but carries no extra information. The associator absorbs endpoint defects enough to create a bicategory, yet cannot turn unequal endpoints into equal ones.

A first extension replaces unique $2$-cells by automorphisms valued in an abelian group $A$. Associators then become choices, the pentagon is expected to become a normalized $3$-cocycle equation, and coherent changes of associator should act by $3$-coboundaries.

For one-object bicategories whose $1$-cells form a group, the obstruction to strictification on fixed $1$-cells should define a third group-cohomology class. The codiscrete case has trivial automorphism coefficients, leaving endpoint associativity as the visible obstruction.

Strictification up to biequivalence should be contrasted systematically with strictness on fixed data. Higher analogues may introduce $3$-cells witnessing coherence among associators. Finally, finite unital magmas can be enumerated, their defect densities classified, and richer $2$-cell labelings searched for cocycles and coboundaries.

\section{Conclusion}

Every unital magma supports a one-object bicategory when its hom-category is codiscrete. Arbitrary associativity defects are bridged by unique invertible associators, and coherence follows from uniqueness. Strictness exists on the fixed data exactly when multiplication is associative.

The twisted natural-number product makes the distinction explicit: $0$ is a unit, but the two bracketings of three copies of $1$ are $5$ and $7$. A coherent reversible comparison connects them, while their inequality prevents strictness. Weak composition does not erase algebraic defects; it relocates them into a higher dimension and controls them through coherence.

\end{document}

